# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows, where git's core.autocrlf=true converts
# LF to CRLF on checkout. That is fine for most files and actively harmful for
# two kinds of file here:
#
#   1. The OKF vault (data/okf_vault/**.md). `app/okf/vault.py:write_note()`
#      always writes LF. `app.okf.verify_migration` proves the migration lost
#      nothing by regenerating each note and asserting it is BYTE-IDENTICAL to
#      the one on disk. On a fresh clone with autocrlf=true, the checked-out
#      note would be CRLF and the regenerated one LF, so every note would fail
#      that check for a reason that has nothing to do with migration
#      correctness.
#
#   2. Worse, and quietly: a stray \r inside a YAML block scalar (the `summary:
#      >` and `rate_text:` fields) becomes part of the parsed string. That
#      string is a verbatim policy figure which `verify_policy_data.py`
#      cross-checks against text extracted from the source PDF. A CRLF
#      round-trip can therefore turn a verified figure into an unverifiable
#      one without anyone editing it.
#
# So: text files are LF in the working tree as well as in the repo.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries -- never touch these.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.sqlite binary
